  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Increasing chances for rain later this afternoon/ \nevening for WV and for areas south overnight tonight through\nMonday morning. After that, no rain looks to be in the forecast\nfor at least a week. \n\nSatellite and analysis date shows sunny skies under flat zonal\nflow for the southern half of the forecast area, with \ntroughing gradually building in across the northern half. This \ngently curving trough/near zonal flow is causing a front to our \nNW to move very slowly today. A much stronger short wave over \nsouthern Canada was helping to kick the front and associated \nrain farther south with time. However, it will a while to \novercome the deep dry air in place above 4kft (see this \nmorning's KRNK sounding). \n\nBetween the dry air and the slow front (not mutually exclusive), \nhave brought rain just into areas north of I-81 this evening, \nbut hold off on much more southward movement until closer to \ntomorrow morning as another short wave ejects out of western \nCanada and the intermountain west. This wave will dig south \nfarther and will support rain over a larger area. The front will\nstall near the NC/SC border tomorrow afternoon. By this point a\ncold air damming pattern will have developed, and overrunning \nwarm air over the cooler air at the surface will keep showers in\nthe forecast, and perhaps a storm or two along our western and \nfar southern counties. \n\nRain chances peak Sunday with the passage of a vorticity ribbon\non the front side of the mid level trough along with the \npassage of the mid level axis and a secondary cold front. This \nboundary will usher in cooler and much drier air, bringing an \nend to our rain chances. \n\nRainfall amounts look to be a half to just over an inch for the \nentire event, but some CAMs show more spotty/lighter amounts \nwith some areas possibly missing out or only receiving drizzle \nor a light shower. Some of the rain could be moderate at times, \nespecially with slow-moving surface boundaries that will \nencourage training due to the mid level flow. However, after \ntalking with WPC we have removed most of the Marginal Risk for \nflooding, given we are in a drought, and will need much more \nrain to cause flooding than we are expecting.\n\nAfter that, no rain looks to be in the forecast for at least a\nweek.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A cold air wedge builds in for the weekend. After \nthat, crisp fall weather with cool temperatures. \n\nA wedge pattern begins to develop Saturday and becomes more \npronounced through Sunday, eventually being eroded by the large\npush of high pressure and cool, dry air behind the secondary \nfront Monday. Temperatures will drop into the 60s and 70s \nareawide Saturday through the next work week. A slight warming \ntrend brings Wednesday so we should see fewer 60s and more 70s \nfor the end of the work week. Sunny skies will go some way to \nmaking it more of a pleasant fall weather stretch. here may be a\nnight or two where lows slip into the 30s in the mountain \nvalleys with patchy frost possible.",
